-- title:
--   A non-generic point on a Čerednik–Drinfeld generic fibre
-- statement:
--   The setting is a Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation datum, assembled from the following groups of data and hypotheses.
--
--   **The base ring.** A prime $r$; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hπ`), completeness of $\mathcal O$ for the $(\pi)$-adic topology (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`), and absolute unramifiedness in the form $(r)=(\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is the fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The unramified coefficient ring.** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (`Onr`) which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, such that $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **The determinant valuation.** A group homomorphism $v_{\det}\colon \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v_{\det}(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The group datum.** A group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma\colon G\to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma\le G$ such that some $z\in\Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix $c\cdot 1$ with $v_{\det}(\sigma z)=2$ (`hcent`) and some $w\in\Gamma$ has $v_{\det}(\sigma w)=1$ (`hodd`); a subgroup $\Gamma'$ consisting exactly of those $x\in\Gamma$ with $v_{\det}(\sigma x)$ even (`hΓ'`); and a homomorphism $\rho\colon G\to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ obtained from $\sigma$ by passing to the projective quotient (`hρ`). Three further conditions: virtual torsion-freeness (`hvtf`), namely a subgroup $\Gamma''\le\Gamma'$ of nonzero relative index in $\Gamma'$ all of whose elements of finite order in $\rho(\Gamma'')$ are trivial; discreteness (`hdisc`), namely for every vertex $v$ of the Bruhat–Tits lattice tree of $\mathcal O\subset K_0$ the set of $g\in\rho(\Gamma')$ fixing $v$ is finite; and cocompactness (`hcocpt`), namely a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every $\rho(\Gamma')$-orbit.
--
--   **The scheme and its deformation-theoretic description.** A scheme $\mathcal X$ and a proper flat morphism $f\colon\mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map
--   $$\Theta_B\colon (\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to_{\mathcal O} B)\times \Omega(B)\longrightarrow \{\varphi\colon\operatorname{Spec}B\to\mathcal X \ :\ \varphi \text{ followed by } f \text{ is the structural morphism}\},$$
--   where $\Omega(B)$ is the set of Deligne data over $B$: assignments $M\mapsto \mathrm{line}(M)$ of a $B$-submodule of $B\otimes_{\mathcal O}M$ to each full $\mathcal O$-lattice $M\subset K_0^2$, with invertible quotients, monotone under inclusions of lattices, equivariant for scalar homotheties, and satisfying the nondegeneracy condition of `DeligneDatum` at every prime ideal of $B$. The hypotheses on $\Theta$ are: naturality in $B$ (`hΘnat`); invariance (`hΘinv`) under the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct`, that is, $\Theta_B(x')=\Theta_B(x)$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma$, $x'_1 = x_1\circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v_{\det}(\sigma\gamma)}$ and $x'_2$ is the pullback of $x_2$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$; exactness on geometric fibres (`hΘfib`), that is, for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi\colon\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to_{\mathcal O}k$, every $k$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ is $\Theta_k(\psi,P)$ for some Deligne datum $P$, and $\Theta_k(\psi,P)=\Theta_k(\psi,P')$ if and only if $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ are related by the twisted action of some $\sigma\gamma$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma$; and universality (`hΘuniv`), that is, for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of maps on nilpotent points that is natural and $\Gamma$-invariant in the same sense, there is a natural family $u$ from the nilpotent points of $f$ to those of $T$ with $u\circ\Theta=\rho'$, and any natural family $u'$ with $u'\circ\Theta=\rho'$ agrees with $u$ on all nilpotent points.
--
--   **The analytic frame.** An algebraically closed field $C$ which is a $K_0$-algebra, carrying a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$ and complete for it; a commutative ring $R$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra mapping to $C$, with the compatibilities of scalars between $\mathcal O$, $K_0$, $R$ and $C$; a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi$ of $K_0$ in $C$ (an element of $K_0$ whose image has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$, such that the valuation of the image of any nonzero element of $K_0$ is squeezed between a power of that of $\varpi$ and the corresponding inverse power); and `hF : IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`, asserting that $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image exactly the set of elements of valuation $\le 1$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ with image of valuation $\le 1$ are exactly those of $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and the images of $\pi$ and of $\varpi$ in $C$ coincide. Further: an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0\colon\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to R$; the rank-one condition `hrk` that for $x,y\in C$ with $v(x)<1$ and $y\ne 0$ some power $v(x)^n\le v(y)$; the condition `hval` that every nonzero $\varepsilon\in\Gamma_0$ dominates the valuation of some nonzero element of $C$; the tameness condition `htame` that for every vertex $v$ of the tree the cardinality of the stabiliser $\{g\in\rho(\Gamma') : g\cdot v=v\}$, viewed in $C$, has valuation $1$; the exhaustion condition `hex` that every element of the Drinfeld upper half-plane $C\setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$; and the assumption that the ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ ρ` of holomorphic functions on the upper half-plane is a domain.
--
--   **The uniformisation map.** A map $\Phi$ from the set of adic points `AdicPoint K₀ π R` (compatible systems of Deligne data over the rings $R/(\pi^{n+1})$) to the set of $R$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$, subject to the four clauses of `hΦ`: (a) for every adic point $x$ and every $n$, reducing $\Phi(x)$ along $R\to R/(\pi^{n+1})$ gives $\Theta$ applied to the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ reduced modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ and the $n$-th component of $x$; (b) $\Phi$ is surjective; (c) $\Phi(x)=\Phi(x')$ if and only if $x' = x\cdot\sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma\in\Gamma'$; (d) base change along $R\to C$ is injective on $R$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$, and every $C$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ is the base change of such an $R$-point.
--
--   Finally, $s_C\colon\operatorname{Spec}C\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$ (`hsC`), and the fibre product of $f$ along $s_C$ is assumed to be an integral scheme.
--
--   **Conclusion.** The underlying space of $\mathcal X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O}\operatorname{Spec}C$ contains a point distinct from the generic point of that scheme.
--
--   This is the geometric non-triviality step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation package: the generic fibre of the quotient scheme $\mathcal X$ over the algebraically closed complete field $C$ does not consist of its generic point alone, which is what forbids it from being zero-dimensional. It feeds [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_functionField_ne_const_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_functionField_ne_const_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient), where the existence of a second point is converted into a non-constant element of the function field.
